## Benchmark 1: Inserting Entries

The first benchmark measures how long it takes to insert
a large number of key-value pairs into a hash table.

```c
clock_t start = clock();

for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
  tableSet(&table, keys[i], NUMBER_VAL(i));
}

clock_t end = clock();

double seconds =
    (double)(end - start) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC;
```

The keys should be created before starting the timer
so the benchmark measures hash table operations rather
than string allocation.

This test is useful because inserting entries may cause
collisions and require the table to resize. When the table
grows, existing entries must be rehashed and placed into
the new array.

Individual insertions can sometimes be expensive because
of resizing, but insertion is expected to have amortized
constant-time performance.

## Benchmark 2: Looking Up Entries

The second benchmark tests how quickly the hash table can
retrieve values using existing keys.

```c
clock_t start = clock();
int found = 0;

for (int repeat = 0; repeat < 100; repeat++) {
  for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    Value value;

    if (tableGet(&table, keys[i], &value)) {
      found++;
    }
  }
}

clock_t end = clock();

double seconds =
    (double)(end - start) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC;
```

This test uses repeated lookups so the execution time is
large enough to measure more reliably.

I chose this test because hash tables are frequently used
for retrieving values, especially when accessing variables
or object properties.

With a good hash function and a reasonable load factor,
lookups should take approximately constant time on average.

However, collisions may cause the table to examine multiple
buckets before finding the correct entry.

## Benchmark 3: Deleting and Reinserting Entries

The third benchmark measures performance when entries are
repeatedly removed and added back into the table.

```c
clock_t start = clock();

for (int i = 0; i < count; i += 2) {
  tableDelete(&table, keys[i]);
}

for (int i = 0; i < count; i += 2) {
  tableSet(&table, keys[i], NUMBER_VAL(i));
}

clock_t end = clock();

double seconds =
    (double)(end - start) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC;
```

I chose this test because clox uses tombstones to represent
deleted entries.

Tombstones allow the hash table to preserve probing
sequences after deletions, but they can also increase
the number of buckets examined during later operations.

Reinserting entries can reuse tombstone positions.

This benchmark helps evaluate how efficiently the table
handles workloads involving frequent modifications.

## Comparing Different Table Sizes

I would run each benchmark with the following sizes:

- 100 entries
- 1,000 entries
- 10,000 entries

Running the same tests with different table sizes would
help determine how performance changes as the table grows.

All tests should use the same build settings and be run
multiple times to reduce measurement noise.

For each test, I would record the execution time and
calculate the average time per operation.

## Expected Performance

| Test | Expected Behavior |
|------|-------------------|
| Insertion | Approximately O(1) amortized per entry |
| Lookup | Approximately O(1) average per lookup |
| Deletion | Approximately O(1) average per deletion |
| Deletion and reinsertion | May be affected by tombstones and resizing |

These are theoretical expectations based on the hash table
design, not measured benchmark results.

Insertion can occasionally be slower when the table grows
and existing entries must be rehashed.

Lookups are expected to remain relatively efficient,
although collisions can increase probing time.

Deleting entries is usually inexpensive, but a large
number of tombstones can affect future lookups.

The actual performance differences would need to be
confirmed by compiling and running the benchmarks.

These benchmarks are proposed tests. No actual timing
measurements have been collected or verified.
